<PGSNModule>
  <!--
    A compliance case generator for the EU AI Act (Regulation 2024/1689).

    The document is a function of a system record, not a hand-drawn argument:
    swap the import below for a different record and the whole case is
    regenerated, with the branches that record's answers select.

    WHAT IS ARGUED, AND ABOUT WHAT

    The Act classifies by intended purpose, not by product. A product with
    several AI functions is, in the Act's terms, several AI systems, each
    classified and regulated on its own. So the top-level goal decomposes
    into one sub-goal per function, and every function carries its own
    classification, its own obligations and its own evidence.

    THE GUARD CHAIN

    The Act reads as a chain of guarded clauses:

        if prohibited use           then it may not be placed on the market
        elsif high-risk             then Art. 8-17, 43, 48, 49 apply
        elsif limited risk          then Art. 50 applies
        else                        no obligation follows from the risk tier
        (and, orthogonally, if GPAI then Art. 53, plus Art. 55 under systemic risk)

    Reaching a clause means every guard before it was false, and that is a
    claim about the function, not a given. Each guard the argument steps over
    therefore appears as a Defeater on that function's goal - the objection
    "this function might have fallen into the earlier clause" - and the
    objection is in turn defeated by the argument that it does not.

    A guard whose condition is a disjunction is not one objection but as many
    as it has disjuncts, because each disjunct attacks the argument on its
    own. De Morgan,

        not (P1 or ... or Pn)  ==  (not P1) and ... and (not Pn)

    is what says so, and here it is the shape of the defeater list itself
    rather than a strategy nested inside a single objection.

    WHY THERE IS NO OR-NODE

    High-risk status is a disjunction over nine independent routes: Art. 6(1)
    (a product or safety component covered by Annex I harmonisation
    legislation) and the eight areas listed in Annex III. A function can
    satisfy several of them at once - a proctoring function that reads
    biometric data during an examination is listed under both Annex III
    point 1 and point 3 - and an exclusive OR node cannot say so.

    Nothing here branches on the disjunction. Every route is asked
    separately, the answers are folded with `any_true`, and a route that
    applies contributes a classification goal while a route that does not
    contributes a dismissed defeater. Two routes answering yes is not a
    conflict; it is two goals.

    WHO CHOOSES GOAL OR DEFEATER

    The record never chooses. For each route and each prohibited practice it
    states a fact - `applies`, true or false - together with the evidence for
    that fact. The generator derives the shape. A record therefore cannot
    supply both a goal and a defeater for the same provision, and if it
    supplies neither the lookup does not reduce and evaluation stops.

    WHAT THIS CANNOT CHECK

    Every enumeration this document owns - the eight prohibited practices,
    the nine high-risk routes, the eleven high-risk obligations - is checked:
    a record that fails to answer one of them halts the evaluation. The list
    of functions is not owned here; it comes from the record. Nothing in the
    mechanism notices a function that was left out, and the incentive runs
    the wrong way, since declaring a prohibited function is what stops the
    case from being produced.

    Completeness of that list is therefore assumed. It cannot be verified
    from inside, because the record is all the generator has. What can be
    done is to make the assumption impossible to skip: it is stated as a
    goal, not an assumption node, and the evidence key it rests on is
    mandatory like any other. See `inventoryGoal`.
  -->

  <!-- The system record is a parameter, so the same generator runs against
       any record that answers what it asks. `case_*.xml` supplies one, and
       swapping records is what shows a case being regenerated rather than
       edited. -->
  <param name="system"/>

  <from file="gsn_templates.xml">
    <import name="any_true"/>
    <import name="concat_all"/>
    <import name="concat_map"/>
    <import name="contestedGoal"/>
    <import name="dismissedGuard"/>
  </from>

  <from file="act_templates.xml">
    <import name="provision"/>
    <import name="obligation"/>
    <import name="prohibitedGuardOrStop"/>
    <import name="routeGuardOrNothing"/>
    <import name="routeGoalOrNothing"/>
    <import name="routeApplies"/>
    <import name="obligationGoal"/>
  </from>

  <def name="systemName"><get key="name" of="system"/></def>

  <!-- ================================================================
       Art. 5: prohibited practices (all eight).

       Dismissal only. There is no "applies, and is complied with" for a
       prohibited practice: a function that deploys one may not be placed on
       the market, and no compliance argument exists to be written. A record
       that answers `applies` with true therefore routes to `undefined`, and
       the evaluation stops on that function's Art. 5 list rather than
       producing a case with a prohibited practice quietly inside it.
  ================================================================ -->
  <def name="prohibitedPractices" as="ol">
    <li><apply template="provision"><arg>Art. 5(1)(a)</arg><arg>subliminal, purposefully manipulative or deceptive techniques</arg></apply></li>
    <li><apply template="provision"><arg>Art. 5(1)(b)</arg><arg>exploitation of vulnerabilities of age, disability or a specific social or economic situation</arg></apply></li>
    <li><apply template="provision"><arg>Art. 5(1)(c)</arg><arg>social scoring leading to detrimental or unfavourable treatment</arg></apply></li>
    <li><apply template="provision"><arg>Art. 5(1)(d)</arg><arg>prediction of criminal offences based solely on profiling or personality traits</arg></apply></li>
    <li><apply template="provision"><arg>Art. 5(1)(e)</arg><arg>untargeted scraping of facial images to create or expand facial recognition databases</arg></apply></li>
    <li><apply template="provision"><arg>Art. 5(1)(f)</arg><arg>inference of emotions in the workplace or in education institutions</arg></apply></li>
    <li><apply template="provision"><arg>Art. 5(1)(g)</arg><arg>biometric categorisation deducing race, political opinions, trade union membership, religious or philosophical beliefs, sex life or sexual orientation</arg></apply></li>
    <li><apply template="provision"><arg>Art. 5(1)(h)</arg><arg>real-time remote biometric identification in publicly accessible spaces for law enforcement</arg></apply></li>
  </def>

  <!-- ================================================================
       High-risk routes: Art. 6(1) and the eight areas of Annex III.

       Nine independent disjuncts, folded rather than branched. The first is
       a different legal route from the other eight, which is exactly why an
       exclusive choice would be wrong: a product covered by Annex I can also
       be listed in Annex III.
  ================================================================ -->
  <def name="highRiskRoutes" as="ol">
    <li><apply template="provision"><arg>Art. 6(1)</arg><arg>a product, or the safety component of a product, covered by the Union harmonisation legislation listed in Annex I and required to undergo third-party conformity assessment</arg></apply></li>
    <li><apply template="provision"><arg>Annex III, point 1</arg><arg>biometrics, so far as permitted under Union or national law</arg></apply></li>
    <li><apply template="provision"><arg>Annex III, point 2</arg><arg>critical infrastructure: safety components in critical digital infrastructure, road traffic, or the supply of water, gas, heating or electricity</arg></apply></li>
    <li><apply template="provision"><arg>Annex III, point 3</arg><arg>education and vocational training</arg></apply></li>
    <li><apply template="provision"><arg>Annex III, point 4</arg><arg>employment, workers management and access to self-employment</arg></apply></li>
    <li><apply template="provision"><arg>Annex III, point 5</arg><arg>access to and enjoyment of essential private services and essential public services and benefits</arg></apply></li>
    <li><apply template="provision"><arg>Annex III, point 6</arg><arg>law enforcement, so far as permitted under Union or national law</arg></apply></li>
    <li><apply template="provision"><arg>Annex III, point 7</arg><arg>migration, asylum and border control management</arg></apply></li>
    <li><apply template="provision"><arg>Annex III, point 8</arg><arg>administration of justice and democratic processes</arg></apply></li>
  </def>

  <!-- ================================================================
       Obligations. All of the same shape: a claim, and the record key that
       carries its evidence. Listing the pairs and mapping over them keeps
       the regulation's text in one place and the plumbing in another.

       Note that obligations do not multiply with the number of routes. A
       function listed under three Annex III points owes Art. 9 once.
  ================================================================ -->
  <def name="highRiskObligations" as="ol">
    <li><apply template="obligation"><arg>Risk management system established and maintained (Art. 9)</arg><arg>risk_management</arg></apply></li>
    <li><apply template="obligation"><arg>Data and data governance requirements met (Art. 10)</arg><arg>data_governance</arg></apply></li>
    <li><apply template="obligation"><arg>Technical documentation drawn up and kept up to date (Art. 11)</arg><arg>technical_documentation</arg></apply></li>
    <li><apply template="obligation"><arg>Automatic logging and record-keeping capability in place (Art. 12)</arg><arg>record_keeping</arg></apply></li>
    <li><apply template="obligation"><arg>Transparency information provided to deployers (Art. 13)</arg><arg>transparency_deployers</arg></apply></li>
    <li><apply template="obligation"><arg>Human oversight mechanisms designed and implemented (Art. 14)</arg><arg>human_oversight</arg></apply></li>
    <li><apply template="obligation"><arg>Appropriate accuracy, robustness, and cybersecurity achieved (Art. 15)</arg><arg>accuracy_robustness</arg></apply></li>
    <li><apply template="obligation"><arg>Quality management system established (Art. 17)</arg><arg>quality_management_system</arg></apply></li>
    <li><apply template="obligation"><arg>Conformity assessment procedure completed (Art. 43)</arg><arg>conformity_assessment</arg></apply></li>
    <li><apply template="obligation"><arg>CE marking affixed (Art. 48)</arg><arg>ce_marking</arg></apply></li>
    <li><apply template="obligation"><arg>EU declaration of conformity drawn up and registered (Art. 49)</arg><arg>declaration_of_conformity</arg></apply></li>
  </def>

  <def name="limitedRiskObligations" as="ol">
    <li><apply template="obligation"><arg>Users are informed they are interacting with an AI system (Art. 50(1))</arg><arg>ai_disclosure_mechanism</arg></apply></li>
    <li><apply template="obligation"><arg>AI-generated or manipulated content is marked as such (Art. 50(2))</arg><arg>ai_content_labeling</arg></apply></li>
  </def>

  <def name="art53Obligations" as="ol">
    <li><apply template="obligation"><arg>Technical documentation for the GPAI model drawn up (Art. 53(1)(a))</arg><arg>gpai_technical_documentation</arg></apply></li>
    <li><apply template="obligation"><arg>Information and documentation provided to downstream providers (Art. 53(1)(b))</arg><arg>gpai_instructions_for_use</arg></apply></li>
    <li><apply template="obligation"><arg>Policy to comply with copyright law established (Art. 53(1)(c))</arg><arg>copyright_policy</arg></apply></li>
    <li><apply template="obligation"><arg>Summary of training content published (Art. 53(1)(d))</arg><arg>training_data_summary</arg></apply></li>
  </def>

  <def name="art55Obligations" as="ol">
    <li><apply template="obligation"><arg>Adversarial testing conducted to identify systemic risks (Art. 55(1)(a))</arg><arg>adversarial_testing</arg></apply></li>
    <li><apply template="obligation"><arg>Serious incident reporting procedure in place (Art. 55(1)(b))</arg><arg>incident_reporting_procedure</arg></apply></li>
    <li><apply template="obligation"><arg>Adequate cybersecurity protection ensured (Art. 55(1)(c))</arg><arg>cybersecurity_measures</arg></apply></li>
  </def>

  <!-- ================================================================
       One function's case.

       `f` is a function record. Everything below reads only from `f`, so
       the same code serves every function of every system.
  ================================================================ -->
  <def name="functionGoal" as="template">
    <param name="f" positional="true"/>

    <def name="fname"><get key="name" of="f"/></def>
    <def name="purpose"><get key="intendedPurpose" of="f"/></def>

    <!-- The disjunction the Act states and GSN cannot draw: high-risk if any
         one of the nine routes applies. A fold, not a branch. -->
    <def name="isHighRisk">
      <apply template="any_true">
        <arg>
          <apply template="map_term">
            <arg><apply template="routeApplies"><arg var="f"/></apply></arg>
            <arg var="highRiskRoutes"/>
          </apply>
        </arg>
      </apply>
    </def>

    <!-- Obligations. The claim comes from the enumeration, the evidence from
         the function record, under the key the enumeration names. -->
    <def name="obligationGoals" as="template">
      <param name="obligations" positional="true"/>
      <apply template="map_term">
        <arg><apply template="obligationGoal"><arg var="f"/></apply></arg>
        <arg var="obligations"/>
      </apply>
    </def>

    <def name="gpaiObligations">
      <if>
        <cond><get key="isSystemicRisk" of="f"/></cond>
        <then><apply template="concat"><arg var="art53Obligations"/><arg var="art55Obligations"/></apply></then>
        <else var="art53Obligations"/>
      </if>
    </def>

    <!-- The risk tier the routes select, and the GPAI obligations that run
         orthogonally to it. -->
    <def name="tierGoals">
      <cases>
        <case>
          <cond var="isHighRisk"/>
          <then>
            <ol>
              <li>
                <Goal>
                  {fname} is properly managed as a high-risk AI system (Art. 8-17, 43-49)
                  <Context>EU AI Act Art. 8-17, 43, 48, 49</Context>
                  <Strategy>
                    Argument over each high-risk provider obligation
                    <subGoals><apply template="obligationGoals"><arg var="highRiskObligations"/></apply></subGoals>
                  </Strategy>
                </Goal>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </then>
        </case>
        <case>
          <cond><get key="isLimitedRisk" of="f"/></cond>
          <then>
            <ol>
              <li>
                <Goal>
                  {fname} meets the transparency obligations for limited-risk AI (Art. 50)
                  <Context>EU AI Act Art. 50</Context>
                  <Strategy>
                    Argument over Art. 50 transparency obligations
                    <subGoals><apply template="obligationGoals"><arg var="limitedRiskObligations"/></apply></subGoals>
                  </Strategy>
                </Goal>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </then>
        </case>
        <else><ol/></else>
      </cases>
    </def>

    <def name="gpaiGoals">
      <if>
        <cond><get key="isGPAI" of="f"/></cond>
        <then>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <Goal>
                {fname} meets its GPAI model obligations
                <Context>EU AI Act Art. 53, 55</Context>
                <Strategy>
                  Argument over each GPAI provider obligation
                  <subGoals><apply template="obligationGoals"><arg var="gpaiObligations"/></apply></subGoals>
                </Strategy>
              </Goal>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </then>
        <else><ol/></else>
      </if>
    </def>

    <!-- A function that is not a GPAI model owes nothing under Art. 53-55,
         but it has to say so. "Is not a GPAI model" is an objection to
         dismiss, not a goal to claim. -->
    <def name="gpaiDefeaters">
      <if>
        <cond><get key="isGPAI" of="f"/></cond>
        <then><ol/></then>
        <else>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <apply template="dismissedGuard">
                <arg>{fname} may be a general-purpose AI model, in which case Art. 53 and, under systemic risk, Art. 55 apply</arg>
                <arg>{fname} is not a general-purpose AI model within the meaning of Art. 3(63)</arg>
                <arg><get key="notGpaiAssessment" of="f"/></arg>
              </apply>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </else>
      </if>
    </def>

    <!-- The strategy is not `immediate`: the step from a classification to the
         duties it brings is what the Act itself says, not an inference too
         obvious to name. Obligations attach to classifications, and the
         classifications this function did not reach are the defeaters. The
         general-purpose model obligations are not part of that chain; they run
         beside it, which is why the strategy names them separately. -->
    <apply template="contestedGoal">
      <arg name="description">{fname} complies with the EU AI Act</arg>
      <arg name="contexts">
        <ol>
          <li><apply template="context"><arg name="description">Intended purpose: {purpose}</arg></apply></li>
        </ol>
      </arg>
      <arg name="strategy">Argument over the function's classification, the obligations the Act attaches to it, and the general-purpose model obligations that apply independently of it</arg>
      <arg name="defeaters"><ol>
        <li><apply template="concat_map"><arg><apply template="prohibitedGuardOrStop"><arg var="f"/></apply></arg><arg var="prohibitedPractices"/></apply></li>
        <li><apply template="concat_map"><arg><apply template="routeGuardOrNothing"><arg var="f"/><arg>Art. 8-17, 43, 48 and 49 apply</arg></apply></arg><arg var="highRiskRoutes"/></apply></li>
        <li var="gpaiDefeaters"/>
      </ol></arg>
      <arg name="subGoals"><ol>
        <li><apply template="concat_map"><arg><apply template="routeGoalOrNothing"><arg var="f"/></apply></arg><arg var="highRiskRoutes"/></apply></li>
        <li var="tierGoals"/>
        <li var="gpaiGoals"/>
      </ol></arg>
    </apply>
  </def>

  <!-- ================================================================
       The boundary of what this document can check.

       Everything the generator enumerates is verified by construction: a
       record that fails to answer halts the evaluation. The list of
       functions is not enumerated here, so its completeness is an
       assumption. It is written as a goal rather than an assumption node so
       that it carries a burden of proof, and the evidence key is mandatory
       like any other, so the claim cannot be silently skipped.
  ================================================================ -->
  <def name="inventoryGoal" as="Goal">
    The AI functions enumerated for {systemName} are all of the AI functions it provides
    <Context>EU AI Act Art. 11 and Annex IV: the technical documentation states the intended purpose and the components of the system</Context>
    <supportedBy><get key="functionInventoryEvidence" of="system"/></supportedBy>
  </def>

  <def name="functionGoals">
    <apply template="map_term">
      <arg var="functionGoal"/>
      <arg><get key="functions" of="system"/></arg>
    </apply>
  </def>

  <def name="case" as="Goal">
    {systemName} complies with the EU AI Act
    <Context>EU AI Act (Regulation 2024/1689)</Context>
    <Strategy>
      Argument over each AI function of the system, and over the completeness of that enumeration
      <subGoals>
        <apply template="concat_all">
          <arg>
            <ol>
              <li><ol><li var="inventoryGoal"/></ol></li>
              <li var="functionGoals"/>
            </ol>
          </arg>
        </apply>
      </subGoals>
    </Strategy>
  </def>

</PGSNModule>
